# ============================================================================
# KATA Architecture - Environment Variables
# ============================================================================
# Copy this file to .env and fill in the values.
# NEVER commit .env to version control!
# Note: Bun loads .env automatically for `bun run` scripts; the agent launch
# wrappers go through dotenv-cli (see the MCP section below).
#
# Three scopes, three banners below (ADR-0005; human guide: docs/core/variables-de-entorno.html):
#   FRAMEWORK            what the boilerplate reads. TEST_ENV has a default; the
#                        rest sit behind a feature switch (Xray sync, Jira host).
#   TOOLING              tools that get their credential elsewhere: CI-only
#                        secrets here; MCP servers that can run at harness level
#                        (web search, Postman) and CLI logins (acli, resend) are
#                        NOT in this file at all.
#   PROJECT-UNDER-TEST   typed EXAMPLES: your app's login, database and API.
#                        Rename or delete them when you adapt the framework.
# Nothing here blocks install, update or the doctor: a value is validated by the
# code that reads it, with a named error, at the point of use.
#
# `bun run test:env:check` (config/validateTestEnv.ts) validates the SHAPE of
# the runtime config (TEST_ENV, the TMS pair when AUTO_SYNC=true). No project
# credential is required up front: the code that needs one fails by name at
# the point of use (config.testUser in config/variables.ts).
#
# The TYPED schema of every variable below lives in `.env.core.schema`
# (generated from cli/lib/variables-manifest.ts, synced) + `.env.schema`
# (project-owned), read by varlock. Validate your .env against it, with
# sensitive values redacted:   bunx varlock load --agent
# ============================================================================


# ============================================================================
# FRAMEWORK: environment selection
# ============================================================================
# Which environment to test against. The valid names are the ones
# config/variables.ts declares (envDataMap); an unknown value fails there by name.
TEST_ENV=local


# ============================================================================
# PROJECT-UNDER-TEST: test user credentials (examples: rename or delete)
# ============================================================================
# The framework never requires these. config/variables.ts maps each
# environment to its pair and config.testUser throws a named error the first
# time a test reads it while the ACTIVE environment's pair is empty. An app
# with no login leaves them empty and drops the ui-setup / api-setup projects.

# --- For TEST_ENV=local ---
LOCAL_USER_EMAIL=
LOCAL_USER_PASSWORD=

# --- For TEST_ENV=staging ---
STAGING_USER_EMAIL=
STAGING_USER_PASSWORD=


# ============================================================================
# FRAMEWORK: browser / runtime (optional overrides)
# ============================================================================
# HEADLESS=true
# DEFAULT_TIMEOUT=30000
# SCREENSHOT_ON_FAILURE=true
# VIDEO_ON_FAILURE=true
# ALLURE_RESULTS_DIR=./allure-results


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#* AI AGENT MCP SERVERS: loading mechanics (three surfaces, one source)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file is the single source of credentials, but no harness reads it
# directly. Each one reads its own surface at startup:
#   - Claude Code  → the `env` block of .claude/settings.local.json (gitignored).
#                    .mcp.json keeps `${VAR_NAME}` placeholders; a MISSING var does
#                    NOT fail at startup (see ADR-0006): the literal string
#                    reaches the server, which dies on its first authenticated call.
#   - OpenCode     → .auth/opencode/<VAR_NAME> value files, referenced from
#                    opencode.jsonc as {file:.auth/opencode/VAR_NAME}. An empty file
#                    substitutes "" (silent). `bun install` creates empty placeholders
#                    so a fresh clone works before `bun run setup` (degraded, not broken).
#   - Codex        → the process environment. .codex/config.toml NAMES variables
#                    (`env_vars`, `bearer_token_env_var`) and reads them at connect
#                    time; a missing one is a HARD error naming the server, so Codex
#                    is the only one of the three that fails loudly and early.
#
# `bun run harness:env` derives the Claude Code and OpenCode surfaces from this
# file (only the vars some MCP uses, never the whole file, never printing values).
# `bun run setup:doctor` reports drift between them. `bun run setup --variables`
# regenerates them on its own at the end.
#
# After filling .env:  1) bun run harness:env   2) restart the agent session
# (MCP servers read credentials at startup; an open session never sees the change).
#
# Launch wrappers (cross-platform, dotenv-cli): bun run claude / bun run opencode /
# bun run codex (Codex Desktop reads this same repo config). Codex needs one of
# these or direnv, because it reads the process environment. direnv is optional:
# only Codex and shell-exported CLI vars (acli, curl, `bun xray`) need it. See .envrc.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ============================================================================
# FRAMEWORK (gated on the Jira host): ATLASSIAN credentials (the HOST is NOT here)
# ============================================================================
# Needed once issue_tracker.atlassian_url is set in .agents/project.yaml, and
# only then: the doctor warns when one is empty behind a set host, never blocks.
# These two cover every Atlassian consumer in this repo:
#   - acli CLI (skill .agents/skills/acli)
#   - xray-cli (skill .agents/skills/xray-cli)
#   - scripts/sync-jira-*.ts (raw REST calls)
#   - cli/doctor.ts auth loop
#   - Jira-Direct TMS provider (config/variables.ts → config.tms.jira)
#   - (opt-in) MCP atlassian server — see .agents/skills/agentic-qa-core/references/mcp-atlassian-optin.md
#
# THE SITE URL IS NOT A VARIABLE HERE. It lives in
#   .agents/project.yaml  ->  issue_tracker.atlassian_url
# and nowhere else locally. Reason: a stale ATLASSIAN_URL inherited from the
# PROCESS environment silently shadows this file (both bun's autoload and
# dotenv-cli skip a var that is already set, and a restart re-inherits it), and
# `jira:sync-issues` then rebuilt .context/PBI/ from a DEAD Jira site with exit
# code 0 and no warning. The host is project identity, so it is anchored to a
# versioned file that shows up in a diff. The test runtime resolves it the same
# way, so a Jira-Direct TMS sync cannot be misdirected either.
#
#   Read it:   bun run --silent jira:url          -> https://acme.atlassian.net
#              bun run --silent jira:url --slug   -> acme.atlassian.net
#   Set it:    bun run agents:setup               (or the installer prompt)
#
# Naming rule:
#   - ATLASSIAN_*  = credentials
#   - JIRA_*       = operational (project key, output dir, custom field, etc.)
#
# Get an API token: https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens
ATLASSIAN_EMAIL=
ATLASSIAN_API_TOKEN=

# --- Jira operational params (NOT credentials) ---
# Default project key for `bun run jira:sync-issues` (else .agents/project.yaml).
# JIRA_PROJECT_KEY=
# Output directory for synced issues (default: .context/PBI).
# JIRA_SYNC_OUTPUT=
# Default sprint selector for `pull --sprint` (active | current | closed | >=N | 7,8,10).
# Flag --sprint overrides this; this overrides the no-filter default.
# JIRA_SYNC_SPRINTS=
# Default csv of optional coverable work-type slugs added to `pull` (e.g. improvement,tech-story,tech-debt).
# Flag --types overrides this; this overrides the default scope (Epics + Stories + Bugs).
# JIRA_SYNC_TYPES=
# Jira-Direct TMS provider — custom field for run status. Optional: when unset it
# resolves from .agents/jira-fields.json -> `test_status`. Set it only to override
# that catalog. Never commit a concrete customfield id outside the catalog.
# JIRA_TEST_STATUS_FIELD=


# ============================================================================
# FRAMEWORK (gated on AUTO_SYNC + TMS_PROVIDER=xray): Xray Cloud, also CI secrets
# ============================================================================
# TMS_PROVIDER=xray
#
# The vars below with a `github` destination in cli/lib/variables-manifest.ts
# are CI secrets referenced by the env block of .github/workflows/regression.yml;
# they are pushed to GitHub Actions by `bun run setup --variables` and read back
# here for local runs. The `local`-only ones are referenced by no workflow.
# Leave them empty unless you actually use Xray sync — they are only required
# when TMS_PROVIDER=xray AND AUTO_SYNC=true.

# Operational flag — the master switch for the results write-back.
#   true  -> `bun run test:sync` after the run imports reports/atc_results.json
#            into the TMS (the global teardown only prints the coverage summary),
#            and .github/workflows/regression.yml imports test-results/junit.xml.
#   false -> NOTHING is written back. The run still passes and still publishes
#            Allure; it just leaves no trace in Jira/Xray. Teardown and CI both
#            say so out loud instead of no-op'ing in silence.
AUTO_SYNC=false
# Xray Cloud API credentials (https://app.getxray.app → API Keys).
XRAY_CLIENT_ID=
XRAY_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Xray project key to sync against. Local-only: referenced by no workflow.
XRAY_PROJECT_KEY=
# Key of the Test Execution this run imports into (e.g. PROJ-194): the RTR by
# default (`RTR: {env}-{date}: Regression Testing`, one per regression verdict,
# created by /regression-testing before the CI trigger and linked to the RTP),
# the sprint-close STR only when the run IS the sprint close. NEVER a Plan key
# (RTP or STP): a Test Plan derives its status from its Executions and is never
# written into. The item is already parented to the "QA Test Artifacts" epic and
# already carries its Test Environment. In CI the regression workflow's
# `execution_key` dispatch input overrides this secret, so the secret is really
# the fallback for a scheduled run. The NAME predates the RTR and is kept so
# downstream secrets keep working. Read only when TMS_PROVIDER=xray: Modality
# jira-native has no Test Executions at all. Leave it empty and every run mints
# a BRAND NEW Test Execution instead: Xray's import API has no parent field (its
# `info` schema is closed), so such an item can never be attached to the QA epic
# afterwards. CI refuses to import without this key for exactly that reason; the
# in-process sync warns and falls back to creating one. Not a secret: a Jira
# issue key.
STP_EXECUTION_KEY=
# Key of the RTP (the long-lived "RTP: <PROJECT>: Regression Test Plan"). Local-only
# and optional: read by the in-process Xray fallback ONLY, so an Execution minted
# by `bun run test:sync` without an execution key is at least linked to the plan.
RTP_KEY=


# ============================================================================
# PROJECT-UNDER-TEST: API exploration & testing (read by the local openapi MCP)
# ============================================================================
# OpenAPI MCP — SCHEMA-READ-ONLY endpoint/contract discovery (list-api-endpoints,
# get-api-endpoint-schema). It does NOT execute authenticated requests — those
# run via curl (see the api:login token flow below).
#   API_BASE_URL       Base URL the agent uses for curl execution (the backend
#                      under test). Also the MCP's request base.
#   OPENAPI_SPEC_PATH  Where the MCP reads the schema: a local file (e.g.
#                      ./api/openapi.json, synced by `bun run api:sync`) OR a
#                      live URL (e.g. http://localhost:3000/api/openapi). The
#                      schema is typically dev/latest — keep that in mind when
#                      the target env (e.g. staging) lags behind dev.
API_BASE_URL=
OPENAPI_SPEC_PATH=

# Agentic API-testing token (curl flow):
#   `bun run api:login [env] [--role <role>]` authenticates and writes the token
#   to .auth/tokens.env (sourceable: export API_TOKEN_<ROLE>_<ENV>='<token>') and
#   .auth/tokens.json (metadata). Both are gitignored. Execute requests with:
#     source .auth/tokens.env && \
#     curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $API_TOKEN_<ROLE>_<ENV>" "$API_BASE_URL/<path>"
#   No restart is needed (the token never enters an MCP).


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#* TOOLING that is NOT in this file (on purpose)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web search and Postman are remote MCP servers whose only project-side content
# was an API key. They are connected at HARNESS level, once per machine, and the
# skills resolve them by capability whatever the server prefix:
#   Claude Code  -> a claude.ai connector, or a user-scope server (`claude mcp
#                   add --scope user ...`, stored in ~/.claude.json)
#   OpenCode     -> the global config, ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json
#   Codex        -> `codex mcp add ...`, stored in ~/.codex/config.toml
# CLIs authenticate themselves and keep their own session:
#   acli    -> echo "$ATLASSIAN_API_TOKEN" | acli jira auth login --site ... --email ... --token
#   resend  -> resend login   (keychain-backed; RESEND_API_KEY is not read by this repo)
# `bun run setup:doctor` reports which of these it can see in your user-level
# harness config; a cloud connector is not detectable and it says so.


# ============================================================================
# PROJECT-UNDER-TEST: DBHUB MCP, database connection (optional, manual setup)
# ============================================================================
# NOT managed by the installer or `bun run setup:doctor` — you must edit `dbhub.toml`
# yourself based on the target project's database. The installer cannot know
# which DB (sqlserver/postgres/mysql/sqlite/mariadb) your project uses.
#
# Required only when the DBHub MCP server is enabled (.mcp.json → "dbhub").
# These vars are interpolated into the tracked file `dbhub.toml` at the repo
# root (the file uses `${VAR}` placeholders — safe to commit, no secrets in
# the TOML itself).
#
# IMPORTANT CAVEAT: DBHub silently substitutes the literal string `${VAR}`
# when a variable is missing from the process environment. This produces a
# cryptic auth failure (not a startup error). Verify all DBHUB_* vars below
# are exported BEFORE launching the MCP server: `env | grep DBHUB`.
#
# Add additional [[sources]] blocks in dbhub.toml if you need more databases.
DBHUB_TYPE=          # sqlserver | postgres | mysql | sqlite | mariadb
DBHUB_HOST=
DBHUB_PORT=          # 1433 (sqlserver) | 5432 (postgres) | 3306 (mysql/mariadb)
DBHUB_DATABASE=
DBHUB_USER=
DBHUB_PASSWORD=


# ============================================================================
# TOOLING: CI-ONLY SECRETS (set in GitHub Actions, NOT needed for local runs)
# ============================================================================
# Pushed to GitHub Actions by `bun run setup --variables`. Optional; only the
# CI workflows consume them, so they stay commented in your local .env.
#
# Slack incoming webhook for CI test-result notifications.
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
#
# Private report hosting (Test Report Portal + Cloudflare R2). The suite
# workflows publish to the portal iff PORTAL_URL is set, else to GitHub Pages;
# scripts/ci/publish-allure-portal.ts throws by name for any it misses. Runbook: .agents/skills/regression-testing/references/private-hosting-setup.md
# PORTAL_URL=
# PORTAL_PROJECT=
# PORTAL_API_KEY=
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2_BUCKET=


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI/CD VARIABLES (set automatically by the runner, NOT here)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI=true          (set by GitHub Actions)
# BUILD_ID=<id>    (set by GitHub Actions)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
